<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    What actually shipped.

    Every PackageReference here is a minimum-version range, so the graph is decided at
    restore time rather than by this repository — 33 packages resolved, including native
    payloads that go inside the signed artifact (SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.*,
    HarfBuzzSharp.NativeAssets.*, Avalonia.Angle.Windows.Natives, Tmds.DBus.Protocol) that
    no .csproj names. Without a lock file there is no record of which versions a given
    release was built from, so a compromise is neither detectable afterwards nor
    reproducible.

    Locked mode only under CI, which GitHub Actions signals with CI=true. Updating a package
    locally should rewrite the lock file as a matter of course; a release build should fail
    rather than quietly resolve something the lock does not name.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    NuGetAudit is on by default in the .NET 10 SDK, but it reports NU1901-NU1904 as
    warnings — and the release publish ran with `-v quiet >/dev/null`, so a known-vulnerable
    transitive was a warning written to a discarded log in the job that ships. Promoted to
    errors so it stops something instead.

    `all` rather than the default `direct`, because the packages most likely to carry an
    advisory here are exactly the ones nothing names: the native asset packages arrive
    transitively through Avalonia.

    The cost is real and deliberate: a newly published advisory against anything in the
    graph breaks the build until it is dealt with, including locally. That is the point —
    the alternative is finding out from the log of a release that already happened.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
